Subject: [#94-1-678] Cars and cities

> 
> Yes, but the reality of city life is traffic jams on 101, yellowish stinky
> air, and no place to park.  I remember the freedom I felt in Boston when
> I didn't have to have a car, I could just walk or T everywhere.  I didn't
> have to pay insurance, I didn't have to park, I didn't have to worry about
> breakdowns, and it usually got me there faster.

If you haven't already, I recommend the book 'Edge City' to understand the
changes occuring in city designs as a response to cars.  Older cities
(like New York) were laid out with a transit system and population density
that matched each other, but were both high.  Adding cars to the situation
didn't help because the older part of the city was too densly laid out,
and there wasn't enough places to park and drive the vehicles.

Most people prefer a single family home to an apartment block, so when
cars became common, lots of people started moving to the suburbs.  Business
followed somewhat later.  We are still in the transition period, but
Houston, where I happen to be at the moment, is an advanced version of
the 'Edge City' that is developing at varying rates all over the country.
Houston happens to be ahead of most other cities because much of it's
growth occurred recently, a lack of zoning (which impedes change when
present), and flat terrain with no bodies of water or mountains in the
central area to impede growth.

An edge city consists of a set of radial and circumferential highways
that provide high speed movement, with major business concentrated at
highway junctions, and spread along highway routes.  Retail business
is concentrated along the primary streets that come off the highways,
and at regular intervals in between, and suburban tracts fill in the
spaces in between.  It is called an edge city because commerce is
spread over the edges (the roads) rather than being concentrated in
a central business district, as in older cities.

If you look at Houston today, you have the radial highways and a
complete inner loop road (I-610), and 75% of the outer loop
(beltway 8).  Major malls can be found at intersections of the
radial and loop highways.  Already there are nearly as many big
office buildings outside of the downtown core as in it.  And
the people who pour concrete - for highways and house foundations -
are firmly etching this pattern of living into the infrastructure,
after which it will be difficult to change.  Just as the transition
from the older city design to the edge city has been and is a
difficult transition.

Many of the 'problems' that older cities have can be traced to the
development of edge city plans.  For example, the people who could
afford to move to the suburbs did so, taking their property tax
payments with them, beyond the city limits.  So the city gets into
trouble by not having enough revenue.  Also, the older, inner, less
desirable areas have the lower rents and housing prices, so of
course that's where the poor concentrate - and you get 'slum' areas.

Now, if someone wants to live in a central city area - to be close to
cultural attractions or nightlife or whatever - they are simply
going to have to put up with the fact that central areas weren't laid
out with cars in mind - so the streets and parking are inadequate for
today's society where most people have cars.

Maybe in a few years technology will provide a solution.  Since
most inner city cars aren't used very much of the time, it costs a
lot to own one for the use you get out of it.  Imagine instead that
a car rental company had a car parked on every block, and that you
could rent it for a trip at low cost by sticking your credit card
in a slot in the car (which unlocks the door).  When you get to
the destination, you park the car and forget it, when you need
to get back, you pick up whatever car is nearest at the time.

Your personal digital assistant talks to the car rental computers
and can tell you where their nearest parked vehicle is located.
The car rental companies get to have their vehicles utilized 
a lot - which is good business, and you get to use a car and pay
for it by usage - rather than the fixed yearly cost for depreciation
maintenance, insurance, etc. you have to pay now.  What is
required is to take the humans out of the rental process, so the
cost per rental can be brought down.

