Yes, there's plenty of evidence for Risk Compensation Theory; naturally
the health fascists have fought tooth and nail to suppress it.  The UK
government and seat belt lobbyists were originally claiming that
compulsion would save 1,000 lives annually.  In fact, the seat belt laws
in Britain either increased total fatalities on the roads, or at best had
little impact.  And there was a sizeable increase in collisions and damage
to vehicles -- car body repairers had a smashing *(i) increase in trade.  

Risk Compensation Theory provides a fine example of unintended
consequences, instinctively dismissed as "irrelevant " or "conjecture" by
socialists, but clearly recognised as significant by those such as the
Austrian praxeological school. 

Year:           1971     1982     1983     1984     1985

Total
deaths:         6999     5705     5436     5599     5788

Expectation
from longterm                     5600     5497     5396 
'71-'82 trend

Government's                               4497     4396 
prediction        

(Background for international readers:  The compulsory seat belt laws
came into effect in the UK at the end of January, 1983, applying to
drivers and front seat passengers.  Compulsory wearing for rear seat
passengers was introduced more recently.)
     
I've taken the compounded annualised mortality decline of around 1.84%,
although I remember hearing of several annual reductions well above the
linear average decline of 118 p.a. just before the laws were introduced
-- I think around 250 was quite common.  The nanny state advocates kept
very quiet about already diminishing casualties due to better brakes and
tires, more motorways (freeways) and by-passes, etc.  In later years
fatalities decreased, again in accordance with the longterm trend,
although the total was still higher than would have been the case without
the 84-85 reversal.

I don't have much data on injury figures, but 1984 showed a 5% increase
on 1983.

The 1983 figures showed a decline of about 3% greater than expected from
the longterm trend.  But this was the year when a massive crackdown on
drink driving was launched.  Drunk drivers were now getting thrown into
jail, with the maximum publicity --  e.g. adverts about Xmas spent in a
police cell.  The evidential breathalyser was introduced in May.  With 33%
more convictions for drink driving, many offenders lost their licenses
throughout 1983.  A further breakdown of the figures may shed some more
light on this.  Between 1982 and 1983, deaths dropped only 3% between
0400 and 2200 hours, but by 23% between 2200 and 0400 hours.

In 1984 the police began to enforce the seat belt laws much more
rigorously, but backed off drink driving.  The *Sunday Telegraph*
(April 21, 1985) reported a sharp increase in the number of unbelted
motorists being prosecuted.  In some areas of England and Wales there
were twice as many prosecutions in 1984 as in 1983.  This occured in
spite of the inability of the Police to attend to other matters like the
drink driving campaign, because of the effects of the miners' strike.

Around 1985 there was a sudden increase in motor insurance premiums, of
well above the inflation rate.  The reasons cited were an increase in
the frequency and size of claims.  *Money Management* (November 1985
issue) ran an article surveying private car insurance.  This included a
statement by the then chairman of the Association of British Insurers, 
Ron Bishop, explaining why the insurance companies had experienced
terrible UK trading results for 1984.  One reason given was "unwise
sometimes insane, competition" but it also said: "Bishop reports that
after years when motor accident frequency has increased only to a small
extent, the latter part of 1984 and early 1985 saw a sudden increase in
incidence which remains unexplained."

According to the *Newbury Weekly Advertiser* 4/4/85, Mr Arthur Mayes, 
coroner at the inquest of a 34-year old doctor observed that: "This is
one of those rare occasions when the wearing of a seat-belt may have
contributed to the death."  Or rather, one of those rare occasions when
an official had the guts to publicly say what he thought.  In another
inquest, this one held before compulsion, (also mentioned in the CIPS
[Choice In Personal Safety] newsletter) a coroner added a rider to the
effect that a driver was strangled by his seat belt and would have been
thrown clear had he not worn one.  Michael Kemp (*Daily Mail* June 28,
1985) wrote that: "Cases of hangman's fracture .... are appearing in car
accidents.  The cause, say doctors in Canada, France, and Britain, is
linked to compulsory seat-belt wearing."

Several other types of injury showed an increase, too.  Dr. John Budd,
casualty officer at Addenbrooke's Hospital, Cambridge, completed a study
which showed a threefold increase in breastbone injuries since belt
legislation.  The *Daily Telegraph* (August 22, 1985) stated: "Car drivers
have suffered an increase in brain injuries and skull fractures since seat
belts became compulsory in January 1983 a Dept of Health report says
today."  The said report (apparently Medical Guidelines for the Issuing
of Exemption Certificates) included: "in the case of drivers severe brain
injuries, fractures of the face and skull and some minor injuries of the
scalp appear to have increased."  I've also read about increases in
whiplash injuries, but don't have references at present.

The *Telegraph* (October 22, 1985) reported on a crash on the M6, in
which two front seat car occupants were burnt alive: "Mr Frederick
Williams who was in a car just ahead of the pile-up said: 'I got out and
