-------------------Taken from *New Scientist* 25 April 1985:-------------- 

                  Belted drivers are killing more pedestrians


   The number of pedestrians and cyclists killed on the road has increased
since car drivers have been compelled to wear seat belts.

   Last week, the DoT published details of road deaths for a 20-month
period from February 1983, when seat belts were made compulsory for people
in the front seats of cars, to September 1984.  They show that the number
of car drivers killed fell by 421, compared with the corresponding period
in 1981 and 1982.  The number of front-seat passengers who were killed
also fell, by 235.  However, the number of pedestrians killed by cars in
the same period rose by 77 and the number of pedal cyclists killed rose by
63.  This unwanted effect of the seat-belt laws was predicted in 1981 by a
paper prepared at the Department of Transport (DoT).  The paper was
suppressed because of its uncomfortable conclusions, and became public
earlier this year only after details were published in New Scientist
(7 February, p. 7).

   This increase in pedestrian deaths was first forecast by John Adams, a
geography lecturer at University College, London.  He argued that forcing
drivers to wear belts would make them feel safer.  As a result they would
drive more dangerously, in much the same way as a trapeze artist might
perform more daring tricks with a safety net than without one.  The result
would be an increase in the number of pedestrians and pedal cyclists being
hit by cars.  Adams' forecast was supported by the DoT's paper, which
described the likelihood of an increase in pedestrian deaths as "alarming".

   On the face of it, the figures seem to show that the lives of 400 car
occupants are being saved annually by compulsory belting up.  This figure
is under half the original government forecast of a saving of 1000 lives
every year.

   However, the meaning of these figures is likely to be hotly disputed.
The government has set up two inquiries into the interpretation of the
number.  One is an internal inquiry at the DoT, and the second is an
independent inquiry, headed by James Durbin, a statistician from the
London School of Economics.

   The number of road accidents fluctuates for a variety of reasons,
including the volume of traffic and the weather, as well as changes in
the rules of the road.  The fall in deaths among the occupants of front
seats in cars is probably related to the changed rules on seat belts.

   But there is uncertainty.  Some passengers may have moved to the back
seat to avoid wearing belts.  Over the same period the number of rear-seat
passengers killed rose by 69. 

   The increase in pedestrian deaths is also likely to be significant. 
The number of pedestrians killed declined by 1000 in the 10 years prior
to the introduction of the new seat-belt law.  This steady improvement 
was reversed only in 1983, which was the year when compulsory belts were
introduced.
